  "text": "ству medicine \n\npaque\n\npaque\npaque we can approach this problem through the following steps: \n\n1. Start by calculating the values of $x_n$ for the first few values of $k$.\n2. Observe the pattern in the values of $x_n$.\n3. Use the pattern to find the value of the infinite product.\n\nLet's start by calculating the values of $x_n$ for the first few values of $k$:\n\n$x_1=2$\n$x_2=\\frac{1}{x_1} + x_1=\\frac{1}{2} + 2=\\frac{5}{2}$\n$x_3=\\frac{1}{x_2} + x_2=\\frac{2}{5} + \\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{29}{10}$\n$x_4=\\frac{1}{x_3} + x_3=\\frac{10}{29} + \\frac{29}{10}=\\frac{841}{290}$\n...\n\nWe can see that the values of $x_n$ seem to be getting larger and larger. \n\nNow, let's observe the pattern in the values of $x_n$. We notice that the reciprocal of $x_n$ seems to be getting smaller in magnitude as $n$ increases. \n\nTo see this, let's look at the value of $\\frac{1}{x_n}$ for the first few values of $k$:\n\n$\\frac{1}{x_1}=\\frac{1}{2}$\n$\\frac{1}{x_2}=\\frac{2}{5}$\n$\\frac{1}{x_3}=\\frac{10}{29}$\n$\\frac{1}{x_4}=\\frac{841}{290}$\n...\n\nWe can see that the value of $\\frac{1}{x_n}$ is getting smaller in magnitude as $n$ increases. \n\nNow, let's use this pattern to find the value of the infinite product. We can rewrite the infinite product as follows:\n\n$\\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x_n}\\right)=\\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(\\frac{x_n+1}{x_n}\\right)$\n\nWe can rewrite the infinite product as a telescoping product, where each term cancels out with a previous term:\n\n$\\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(\\frac{x_n+1}{x_n}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{x_1+1}{x_1}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x_2+1}{x_2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x_3+1}{x_3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x_4+1}{x_4}\\right)...$\n\nWe can see that the terms $x_n$ and $x_n+1$ cancel out, leaving us with:\n\n$\\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x_n}\\right)=\\frac{x_1+1}{x_1}$\n\nSince $x_1=2$, we have:\n\n$\\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x_n}\\right)=\\frac{2+1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the value of the infinite product is $\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}$.",
